 Sarah's points. We have our own notes. We need to decide whether to stick with D or switch to B. We need to consider Sarah's concerns: D's weak leadership and arrogance vs B's grumpy/uncooperative. We need to weigh criteria: reliability, stress resilience, technical competence, decision quality, attention, crew cooperation, communication, responsibility, adaptability.\n\nOur own notes: D: responds to unexpected events adequately, concentrates well, solves problems extremely well, takes responsibility seriously. Concerns: know-it-all, hot temper, weak leadership, arrogant. B: very conscientious, handles stress well, good at assessing weather, excellent computer skills, weak memory for numbers, makes nasty remarks, grumpy/uncooperative. A: anticipate dangerous, see complex connections, spatial vision, leadership, but show-off, not open to new ideas. C: correct decisions quickly, calm in crisis, understands complicated tech, difficulty communicating, egocentric, not willing to further education.\n\nWe need to decide. Both D and B have concerns about crew cooperation. D's arrogance and weak leadership may be more severe than B's nasty remarks. But B's weak memory for numbers could affect attention to operational details. D's hot temper also severe. Which is more critical? Probably crew cooperation and leadership are crucial. Both have issues. We need to argue.\n\nWe could maintain D but address Sarah's concerns, perhaps argue that D's responsibility and problem solving outweigh leadership concerns, and that leadership can be trained. Or we could shift to B. Let's see: In our earlier note, we recommended D. Sarah argued for B. We need to respond. We could say we still think D is best because his responsibility, concentration, problem solving, and ability to handle unexpected events are critical for long-distance flights, and his leadership concerns are less severe than B's uncooperative behavior and weak memory for numbers. Also D's hot temper is a concern but can be mitigated. So we keep D.\n\nThus public message: I acknowledge Sarah's points, but I still recommend D, explaining why. Provide evidence: D's responsibility, concentration, problem solving, handling unexpected events. Compare to B's weak memory for numbers and nasty remarks. Argue that D's leadership concerns can be mitigated, while B's memory issue is safety-critical.\n\nProvide metadata JSON: vote Candidate D.\n\nProceed."}
